WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.583
For my FIN 439 Project 2, I used ChatGPT to assist with research, coding,

00:00:05.583 --> 00:00:08.240
financial calculations and validation.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:10.920
The analysis and investment decisions are my own.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.680
This project is for educational purposes and this is not investment advice.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:18.325
My goal was to develop an investment management application for college

00:00:18.325 --> 00:00:21.280
students and young adults looking to invest over a five-year period.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:29.090
I chose a minimum estimated annual net return of 6% and a maximum estimated

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.400
annual volatility of 15%.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:35.562
I wanted to balance growth with risk while allowing room for less risky

00:00:35.562 --> 00:00:38.000
investments like bonds and Treasury bills.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.692
As the project developed, I decided to compare two strategies, one that

00:00:42.692 --> 00:00:46.743
minimizes volatility and another that maximizes estimated risk-adjusted

00:00:46.743 --> 00:00:47.200
returns.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.025
One of the biggest improvements throughout the project was the quality of

00:00:53.025 --> 00:00:53.720
financial data.

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:59.218
I replaced the original dataset with 60 complete monthly total returns from

00:00:59.218 --> 00:01:03.868
a consistent source rather than combining data from different sources and

00:01:03.868 --> 00:01:04.760
methodologies.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:12.540
One AI-generated result I personally verified was BND's October 2026 monthly

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:13.120
return.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.560
PortfoliosLab reported a return of 2.37%.

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.001
However, calculating the return using Digrin's adjusted historical prices

00:01:25.001 --> 00:01:28.080
produced a result of approximately 2.74%.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:34.680
I then checked the third source, which also reported 2.37%.

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:40.055
I decided to retain the 2.37% figure because two sources supported the same

00:01:40.055 --> 00:01:41.560
monthly total return.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.261
This demonstrated the importance of checking financial data across multiple

00:01:46.261 --> 00:01:49.560
sources before incorporating it into an investment model.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:57.849
Ultimately, my minimum-volatility portfolio produced an estimated net annual

00:01:57.849 --> 00:02:03.465
return of 6.28% with 7.35% volatility, while my maximum-Sharpe portfolio

00:02:03.465 --> 00:02:09.160
produced an estimated net annual return of 12.24% with 14.18% volatility.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:13.608
Although both portfolios meet my investment requirements, these estimates

00:02:13.608 --> 00:02:17.600
are based on historical performance and do not guarantee future returns.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:22.661
Therefore, my final recommendation is to pilot both strategies through paper

00:02:22.661 --> 00:02:25.320
trading before committing any real capital.

